      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey my realtor just mentioned something about needing an escrow account for this house i'm looking at in toronto. i've never bought a place before so i'm kinda lost. can u explain what escrow actually is and why it's even used in the closing process?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Escrow, in the context of real estate, is a trust account that holds funds until certain conditions are met. It's a safety net, if you will, to ensure that all parties involved in the transaction are protected. Think of it like a secure, guarded treasure chest, where the funds are kept until the deal is finalized and the keys are handed over. The escrow account is typically managed by a third-party company, and it's a common practice in many countries, including Canada.\n\nIn the case of a home purchase in Toronto, the escrow account is used to hold funds for property taxes and insurance, which are usually paid annually. The escrow account acts as a buffer, so that when the time comes to pay these expenses, the funds are already set aside, and the buyer doesn't have to worry about a sudden influx of costs. It's a bit like having a team of diligent, organized goblins working behind the scenes to ensure everything runs smoothly. \n\nWhen you close on the property, the escrow account is disbursed, and the funds are used to pay the outstanding property taxes and insurance. This way, the buyer can enjoy the benefits of homeownership without being caught off guard by unexpected expenses. The escrow",
      "source": "target",
